The earlier version summed all divisors, including n itself. The team note asks for proper divisors, so I've changed it to exclude n. With that change, 36 gives 55 and 57 gives 23, so the assertion still holds.

```python
def sum_proper_divisors(n):
    """Return the sum of the positive divisors of n, excluding n itself (n >= 1)."""
    total = 0
    i = 1
    while i * i <= n:
        if n % i == 0:
            if i != n:
                total += i
            j = n // i
            if j != i and j != n:
                total += j
        i += 1
    return total


def are_equivalent(a, b):
    """Return True if a and b have the same sum of proper divisors."""
    return sum_proper_divisors(a) == sum_proper_divisors(b)


assert are_equivalent(36, 57) == False
```

